# Stripe Clustering Defense

Stripe runs three layers of automated cross-account detection:

1. Similarity Clustering (2020) — graph of accounts connected by shared bank, email, IP, device, card fingerprint, plus text/embedding similarity.
2. Payments Foundation Model (2025) — self-supervised embedding model trained on tens of billions of transactions; surfaces subtle per-payment signals.
3. Fraudulent Website Signal (2026, LLM-based) — evaluates the registered domain for "deceptive content, misleading product claims, fake storefronts, or content that doesn't match the stated business model."

If any account in a cluster gets confirmed-fraudulent, Stripe's `other_related_accounts` indicator can take down every account in the cluster.

## What this repo enforces (do NOT undo)

- `store/lib/layout-fingerprint.js` — 5 distinct layout variants. Each store's rendered HTML head has unique CSS variable names, nav class names, body class, header class, transition timing, and font stack. Adding a store requires adding a new variant entry.
- `store/scripts/generate-prices.js` — deterministic, per-store unique 24-price vector. NEVER ship a store with prices copied from another store.
- `APPLE_PAY_DOMAIN_ASSOCIATION` env var only (per deploy) — never check the file into the repo. Per Apple Pay Engineering, the file represents a 1:1 binding to the merchant ID; sharing one file across multiple Stripe accounts is a deterministic cluster signal.
- `store/scripts/sterility-check.js` — runs at `prestart`; fails the boot on `gfm`/`cloaked`/`fundrais` and warns on `donation`/`donate`/`donor` outside an explicit allowlist.
- Per-store `metadataKeys` in `config.js` — every store uses different PaymentIntent metadata key names (`studio_ref` vs `vendor_id` vs `outlet_ref`, etc.) so the metadata shape itself isn't a fingerprint.

## What is intentionally tolerated (shared, low-signal)

- Tailwind CDN, Inter font, Google Fonts preconnect — universal across millions of sites.
- Express + Stripe SDK pinned to the same version — server-side, never reaches Stripe.
- Railway hosting (subnet 66.33.22.0/24) — cloud IP is a low-signal feature on its own.
- Supabase project shared across stores — server-side, invisible to Stripe.
- Identical `cart.js` script body served from `store/public/js/cart.js` — script bodies don't fingerprint sites at Stripe's evaluation layer.

## Per-account hygiene (manual, NOT code-enforced)

The code-side mitigations above are necessary but NOT sufficient. The account-vendor / operator must also maintain:

- One AdsPower profile per Stripe dashboard.
- One residential IP (NodeMaven or equivalent) per AdsPower profile.
- Unique ProtonMail/Tutanota account email per Stripe account.
- Different payout bank account per Stripe account.
- `business_profile.url` on the Stripe account MUST match the live storefront domain. `business_profile.support_email` MUST match the storefront's contact email. NEVER accept an aged account where the registered URL points to a previous business.

A rented Stripe account with `email = gmail.com` against a `.shop` storefront triggers Stripe's `owner_email` and `web_presence` indicators automatically. See `docs/CLUSTERING-AUDIT-2026-05.md` for the alzworks-digital case.

## When in doubt

Read `docs/ACCOUNT-ISOLATION.md` (what must rotate per account burn) and `docs/STRIPE-INVIGILATION.md` (what Stripe can see from the wire) before making any change that touches a store's public surface.
